Fix two Rose Tattoo hires-mode bugs specific to Watson's Journal
(TattooJournal): scrambled/staticky colors covering the whole journal, and
(once that was fixed) doubled/ghosted text - crisp text drawn on top of,
rather than instead of, the blocky native bitmap glyphs.

This is a follow-up to rosetattoo-hires-font-ttf-override.patch and
rosetattoo-hires-tooltip-text-fix.patch (which introduced the hires TTF
text overlay and its clear/erase helpers) - the screen.cpp/.h hunks below
add one new helper (clearRoseTattooHiresTextLayer()) and one new guard
clause to queueRoseTattooHiresText() that didn't exist in those patches.

Root cause 1 - color noise/static: unlike TattooMap::show() (which reads
its palette directly into screen._cMap), TattooJournal::show() read the
journal's palette into a *local* byte palette[] array and never updated
screen._cMap. _cMap is what the hires compositor's nearest-neighbor
fallback path (Screen::update()) uses to convert native palette-index
pixels to RGB - with _cMap still holding the previous room's palette while
on-screen pixel indices referred to the journal's own distinct palette,
every hires frame looked up journal pixel indices in the wrong (room)
palette, producing scrambled colors over the whole journal. Fixed by
reading the palette directly into screen._cMap (mirroring
tattoo_map.cpp's convention) and calling screen.setPalette(screen._cMap)
instead of setPalette(palette).

Root cause 2 - doubled/ghosted text: Fonts::writeString() unconditionally
queues hires TTF text via queueRoseTattooHiresText() whenever hires mode is
active, and blendRoseTattooHiresTextLayer() can only mask away the
composite's blocky nearest-neighbor-upscaled bitmap glyphs by repainting
from _roseTattooHiresBackground first - but the journal deliberately
clears that background (it draws its own paper/text UI directly into
_backBuffer1 and has no hires background override of its own), so the
masking step was a no-op, leaving both the blocky upscaled bitmap text AND
the crisp TTF text visible simultaneously. Fixed by skipping
queueRoseTattooHiresText() entirely when no hires background is loaded -
a general fix applicable to any future full-screen overlay with the same
characteristics (draws its own bitmap UI, has no hires background), not
just the journal.

Also clears both persistent hires overlay layers (text and sprite) at the
start of TattooJournal::show(), since - being a full-screen overlay drawn
outside the normal room-widget lifecycle - any glyphs/sprites still queued
from whatever was on screen just before the journal opened (tooltips,
inventory icons, etc.) would otherwise keep being alpha-blended on top of
the journal every frame. This needed a new Screen::clearRoseTattooHiresTextLayer()
method (full-wipe), mirroring the existing clearRoseTattooHiresSpriteLayer().

Debugging note: an MD5 comparison of before/after screenshots proved the
text/sprite-layer clearing alone had zero effect on the reported glitches,
ruling that hypothesis out and redirecting the investigation to the actual
_cMap root cause above.

Root cause 3 - room's palette-cycled animations (candle flicker, shadow
shimmer) blinking with wrong colors after closing the journal: the fix for
root cause 1 above made TattooJournal::show() load the journal's own
palette directly into screen._cMap, but never restored the room's palette
into _cMap afterward. TattooUserInterface::doJournal() (the caller)
restores the visible screen palette right after journal.show() returns via
screen.setPalette(screen._cMap), on the (previously true) assumption that
_cMap still holds the room's palette - true before root cause 1's fix,
since the journal used to load its palette into a separate local buffer
and never touched _cMap. Unlike TattooMap::show(), closing the journal
does not necessarily trigger a Scene::loadScene() reload that would
naturally restore _cMap the normal way. Fixed by saving the room's
palette into a local buffer before overwriting _cMap with the journal's
palette, and restoring it into _cMap right before TattooJournal::show()
returns.

